#pragma once

// `cat::map` is libCat's recommended general purpose map container. It is an
// unordered open-addressing hash map based on `boost::unordered_flat_map`, and
// loans from Chandler Carruth's lecture "A Clean and Minimal Map API".
// https://bannalia.blogspot.com/2022/11/inside-boostunorderedflatmap.html
// https://youtu.be/kye4aD-KvTU
//
// Although it uses some specific terminology proposed by P4139.
//
// There are four fundamental operations to `cat::map`.
//
//    `.operator[](key)`, a "lookup" that returns `cat::maybe<T&>`.
//    The `maybe` is engaged iff such a `key` has been inserted in the hash map
//    previously.
//
//    `.insert(key, value)`, inserts a `key` iff such a `key` has not been
//    inserted in the hash map previously. A `maybe<void>` is returned engaged
//    if `key` has been inserted, or empty if `key` had already been inserted.
//
//    `.update(key, value)`, inserts a `key` iff such a `key` has not been
//    inserted previously, otherwise it updates its corresponding `value`.
//
//    `.remove(key)`, removes a `key` from the hash map iff such a `key` has
//    been inserted previously. A `maybe<void>` is returned engaged if `key` has
//    been removed, or empty if it had not previously been inserted.
//
//    `.contains(key)`, returns a `true` `bool` when `key` has previously been
//    inserted, or `false` when it had not or has already been removed.
//
// Unlike `std::map` family, `.operator[]` is read-only. It will never insert.
// `cat::map` is paramaterized by `cat::map_flags`, which may toggle the
// following features:
//
//  `inline_storage(count)`
//     Store up to `count` entries inside the map, only allocating when its
//     inline capacity is exceeded.
//
// Convenience type aliases are provided for these configurations:
//
//  `cat::map<Key, T>`, a `basic_map` with 4 inline entries.
//  `cat::map_small<Key, T, n>`, a `basic_map` with `n` inline entries.
//  `cat::map_big<Key, T>`, a `basic_map` without inline storage.
//
// There also exist the following non-owning map-like containers.
//
//  `cat::map_ref<Key, T>`, a reference to any `basic_map` regardless of its
//  `map_flags`. It supports every operation of `basic_map` and dispatches
//  statically.
//  `cat::dyn_map<Key, T>`, a type-erased reference to any map that may insert,
//  update, and remove through a `dyn_allocator`.
//  `cat::map_view<Key, T>`, a type-erased view of any map that only looks up
//  keys.
//  `cat::map_view<Key, T const>` also forbids mutating values.
//
// TODO: We need ordered, closed-addressing, sparse, and shared hash maps.

